  "issue_title": "compiler-sfc: recursive type + indexed access makes compileScript walk a cyclic AST (RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded)",
  "issue_body": "### Vue version\n\n```\n3.6.0-rc.2\n```\n\n### Link to minimal reproduction\n\nhttps://play.vuejs.org/#\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nThe repro has four files. `App.vue` reads a recursive type through an indexed access type\n(`TreeNode['parent']`), and `Second.vue` / `Third.vue` are identical components whose props\ntype resolves to that same recursive type.\n\n**After opening the link, click two tabs in the file bar:**\n\n1. Click the **`Second.vue`** tab. It compiles fine.\n2. Click the **`Third.vue`** tab. `compileScript` throws.\n\nThe clicks are only needed because the Playground compiles files lazily, one per active tab.\nWhat matters is the sequence of three `compileScript` calls in a single process:\n\n1. the component using `TreeNode['parent']` (the indexed access)\n2. a component whose props type resolves to `TreeNode`\n3. a second component whose props type resolves to `TreeNode` \u2014 this one throws\n\nThe files:\n\n```ts\n// types.ts\nexport type TreeNode = {\n  parent?: TreeNode;\n};\n\nexport type ItemProps = {\n  node?: TreeNode;\n};\n```\n\n```vue\n<!-- App.vue -->\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { type TreeNode } from './types.ts';\nimport Second from './Second.vue';\nimport Third from './Third.vue';\n\ndefineProps<{ parentNode?: TreeNode['parent'] }>();\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <Second />\n  <Third />\n</template>\n```\n\n```vue\n<!-- Second.vue and Third.vue, identical -->\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { type ItemProps } from './types.ts';\n\ndefineProps<ItemProps>();\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <div />\n</template>\n```\n\nReproduces the same way outside the Playground, in plain Node against\n`@vue/compiler-sfc@3.6.0-rc.2`, by calling `compileScript` on the three files in that order.\nIt also reproduces with the `esm-browser` build, with and without `registerTS`, and with both\na real filesystem and an in-memory `fs` option.\n\n### What is expected?\n\nAll three components compile, as they do on 3.5.40.\n\nSame files, same click sequence, Vue 3.5.40 (control):\nhttps://play.vuejs.org/#\n\n### What is actually happening?\n\n`compileScript` throws:\n\n```\nRangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded\n    at SyncWalker.visit (@vue/compiler-sfc@3.6.0-rc.2/dist/compiler-sfc.esm-browser.js)\n    at SyncWalker.visit (\u2026)\n    at SyncWalker.visit (\u2026)\n    \u2026 ~5400 frames\n```\n\n**Cause.** `resolveTypeReference` caches the resolved declaration on the reference node itself:\n\n```js\nreturn canCache ? (node._resolvedReference = resolved) : resolved\n```\n\n`_resolvedReference` holds an AST node, so it has a `.type` string. `compileScript` then walks\neach top-level `<script setup>` statement with `estree-walker`, whose `visit` iterates **every\nenumerable own key** and recurses into any value with a `.type` string. It therefore follows\n`_resolvedReference` as if it were an AST child. Once a recursive type has had its recursive\nmember resolved, the reference graph contains a back-edge, and the walk never terminates.\n\nInstrumenting the walker on the real-world case gives the closed loop (the last node is already\nan ancestor):\n\n```\n(root):VariableDeclaration\n  -> declarations[0]:VariableDeclarator \"props\"\n  -> init:CallExpression                          // defineProps<Props>()\n  -> typeParameters:TSTypeParameterInstantiation\n  -> params[0]:TSTypeReference \"Props\"\n  -> _resolvedReference:TSIntersectionType\n  -> types[0]:TSTypeReference \"Omit\"\n  -> typeParameters:TSTypeParameterInstantiation\n  -> params[0]:TSTypeReference \"BudgetCreateStepsControlsProps\"\n  -> _resolvedReference:TSTypeLiteral {11 members}\n  -> members[1]:TSPropertySignature \"budget\"\n  -> typeAnnotation:TSTypeAnnotation\n  -> typeAnnotation:TSTypeReference \"Budget\"\n  -> _resolvedReference:TSTypeLiteral {63 members}\n  -> members[45]:TSPropertySignature \"parent\"     // parent?: Budget & { \u2026 }\n  -> typeAnnotation:TSTypeAnnotation\n  -> typeAnnotation:TSIntersectionType\n  -> types[0]:TSTypeReference \"Budget\"            // back to the ancestor above\n```\n\nThe `_resolvedReference` caching line is identical in 3.5.40 and 3.6.0-rc.2, so the caching is\nnot itself new. What changed is that on 3.6 the back-edge is reachable from the statement AST\nthat `compileScript` walks. On 3.5.40 the same file set produces no reachable back-edge.\n\n**Verification.** Skipping that single key in the walk clears it. Patching the walker to ignore\n`_resolvedReference` let all 3896 SFCs in our application compile on 3.6.0-rc.2, where the\nunpatched compiler crashes. Ignoring every `_`-prefixed key works too.\n\nA possible fix is to make these internal caches non-enumerable, for example\n`Object.defineProperty(node, '_resolvedReference', { value: resolved, writable: true, configurable: true, enumerable: false })`,\nso `for\u2026in`-based walkers cannot mistake them for AST children. The same applies to any other\ninternal annotation attached to AST nodes that holds a node.\n\n### System Info\n\n```shell\n  System:\n    OS: macOS 26.6\n    CPU: (10) arm64 Apple M4\n    Memory: 887.94 MB / 16.00 GB\n    Shell: 5.9 - /bin/zsh\n  Binaries:\n    Node: 24.18.0 - /opt/homebrew/opt/node@24/bin/node\n    npm: 11.16.0 - /opt/homebrew/opt/node@24/bin/npm\n    pnpm: 11.15.1 - /opt/homebrew/opt/node@24/bin/pnpm\n  Browsers:\n    Chrome: 150.0.7871.187\n    Firefox: 153.0.1\n    Safari: 26.6\n  npmPackages:\n    vue: 3.6.0-rc.2 => 3.6.0-rc.2\n```\n\n### Any additional comments?\n\nFound while upgrading a Vue 3 application (about 3900 SFCs, Vite 8.1.5,\n`@vitejs/plugin-vue` 6.0.8) from 3.5.40 to 3.6.0-rc.2. The only change was the `vue` version.\n\nIn a real project the failure looks non-deterministic, which made it confusing to diagnose:\n\n- The production build failed on one component, the dev server on a different one. Which file\n  crashes depends on the order in which the shared type-resolution cache gets warmed, not on\n  anything about the file itself.\n- Every crashing component compiles fine on its own. It only fails once other components have\n  been compiled in the same process.\n- In dev, the resulting Vite error overlay then blocks unrelated interactions, so the visible\n  symptom appears in features that have nothing to do with the types involved.\n\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-29T12:49:23Z",
